            <div xml:id="d347">

                <pb facs="0515" n="435" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg435"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.114 St. Pierre-Miquelon/434: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-12-16T19:00:00-05:00">December 16,
                            1936—7 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>530. Your No. 1194, December 4, 5 p.m. The suggestions made by the Foreign Office
                    are presumably in substitution for those we suggested in our telegram No. 475 of
                    November 23, 7 p.m. We would, of course, greatly prefer to see the French
                    Government prohibit the exportation of all alcohols from St. Pierre-Miquelon
                    with the exception of those described in point (1) of your telegram or, if this
                    is impossible, that the exportation thereof be in accordance with the provisions
                    of the decree of April 9, 1935. If this also cannot be achieved, it is believed
                    that the import tax of 15 francs per liter would be preferable to the imposition
                    of a quota.</p>
                <p>With regard to point (2), please endeavor to have the word “foreign” omitted from
                    the proposed decree in order that it may apply to all alcohol now in storage at
                    St. Pierre-Miquelon.</p>
                <pb facs="0516" n="436" xml:id="pg436"/>
                <p>With reference to point (3) it is assumed that the term “alcool de traite” means
                    all alcohol fit for human consumption. Please confirm this understanding.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moore</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
